The SDK files were derived from a field port of AlpaSim (the field team's
PathFollower_MPC folder: mpc/alpasim_mpc.py, mpc/alpasim_nmpc.py,
mpc/mpc_follower.py). That port was made from an upstream revision it did not
record, so these files are NOT verbatim copies of any published AlpaSim
revision.

Upstream AlpaSim sources (per the field port's NOTICE):
  src/controller/alpasim_controller/mpc_impl/nonlinear_mpc.py
  src/controller/alpasim_controller/mpc_impl/linear_mpc.py
  src/controller/alpasim_controller/mpc_controller.py

Derived SDK files and what they came from:
  control/mpc/params.py             <- alpasim_mpc.py (VehicleParams, accel_limits, MPCGains)
                                       and mpc_follower.py (params_from_json)
  control/mpc/models.py             <- alpasim_nmpc.py (_build_model)
  control/mpc/alpasim_nonlinear.py  <- alpasim_nmpc.py (NonlinearMPC)
  control/mpc/alpasim_linear.py     <- alpasim_mpc.py (LinearMPC)
  control/mpc/law.py                <- mpc_follower.py (MPCFollower: reference, solve
                                       cadence, initial state incl. the AlpaSim
                                       coerce_dynamic_state d0 rule, finish)

Modifications by the GenesisVehicle contributors (summary):
  - Korean comments translated/rewritten in English; module docstrings added.
  - Parameters, gains and acceleration limits moved to a numpy-only module, so
    importing them needs neither casadi/do_mpc nor osqp.
  - The do_mpc problem is set up at construction (placeholder reference)
    instead of at the first solve.
  - casadi's NumPy mode and do_mpc's import-time warning filters are scoped to
    the calls that need them instead of being set for the whole process.
  - do_mpc's per-solve history is cleared after every solve.
  - Solve results carry the IPOPT / OSQP status; each solve is classified
    (converged / capped / infeasible / other / exception / non-finite) and a
    policy decides what is applied (control/mpc/law.py).
  - do_mpc's previous-input cost parameter is reset to the measured state
    when the MPC restarts (block entry, after a fallback, after set_path).
  - LinearMPC: QP build and solve under a 1-thread BLAS limit; OSQP setup /
    solve exceptions caught; solve(raise_error=False) passed explicitly.
  - The field follower's reference and finish are reused per forward block of
    a path inside genesis_vehicle.control.PathFollower; reverse blocks are
    driven by the SDK's PD law.
  - Optional solve-time options (shifted warm start, OpenBLAS thread cap,
    extra IPOPT options), all off by default.

The field-authored files (mpc_follower.py, tools/identify.py,
tools/measure_table.py, demo.py) are used in the SDK with the field team's
permission (2026-11-21): control/identify.py, the grid flags of
control/sweep_measure.py and samples/mpc_follow_demo.py derive from them.

Third-party runtime dependencies of the MPC layer (requirements-mpc.txt) are
installed by the user and NOT redistributed with this SDK:
  casadi (LGPL-3.0-or-later; bundles Ipopt (EPL-2.0), MUMPS (CeCILL-C),
  METIS glue (EPL-1.0), OpenBLAS (BSD-style)), do-mpc (LGPL-3.0),
  osqp (Apache-2.0), threadpoolctl (BSD-3-Clause).
